.TH UNROLL 1
.SH NAME
.PP
unroll \- copy only the new photos and videos from an iPhone to a folder on a drive
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
.nf
.RS 4
unroll
unroll run <task> [\-\-dry\-run] [\-\-yes] [\-\-json] [\-\-list]
unroll status [\-\-json]
unroll tasks [list | add | remove]
unroll rebuild\-index [<task>] [\-\-yes]
unroll scan
unroll help [<topic>]
.RE
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
unroll reads photos and videos directly from a connected iPhone or iPad, remembers what it has already copied, and copies only new items into a date\-based folder structure on a drive. Optionally it adds them to Apple Photos as referenced files, so the Photos library stays small while the originals stay in the folder. It never changes or deletes anything on the device.
.PP
Run without arguments in a terminal, unroll opens an interactive menu. Outside a terminal it never shows menus or prompts; without arguments it prints the help.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS run <task>
.PP
Checks that the task's destination and device are connected, compares the device with the destination's index and copies only new items. \fB\-\-dry\-run\fR only shows what would be copied, by month; \fB\-\-list\fR adds every item. \fB\-\-yes\fR confirms a first run (or a run after the task's device, destination or index changed) without asking. \fB\-\-json\fR writes one JSON object per line, ending with \fBresult\fR and the exit code. Ctrl\-C finishes the current file, then stops.
.SS status
.PP
Shows the connected devices and, for each task, whether its device and destination are connected, its last run and the number of files in its destination. \fB\-\-json\fR prints a single object.
.SS tasks
.PP
\fBtasks list\fR shows all tasks. \fBtasks add \-\-name <name> \-\-destination <folder>\fR creates one without the wizard; \fB\-\-device\fR, \fB\-\-folder\-template\fR and \fB\-\-versions\fR are optional. \fBtasks remove <task>\fR deletes a task but never the files or the index in its destination.
.SS rebuild\-index
.PP
Recreates a task's index from the files in its destination. Files are only read. Asks for confirmation unless \fB\-\-yes\fR is given; without a terminal, \fB\-\-yes\fR is required.
.SS scan
.PP
Lists connected devices and the items on them without copying anything. Useful for troubleshooting.
.SS help <topic>
.PP
Shows a help topic: setup, tasks, photos\-referenced, troubleshooting, exit\-codes or keys.
.SH SETTING UP YOUR IPHONE AND MAC
.SS On the iPhone
.IP \(bu 2
Settings → Photos → Transfer to Mac or PC → \fBKeep Originals\fR. Otherwise the iPhone may convert photos to JPEG during the transfer.
.IP \(bu 2
If you use iCloud Photos on the phone, turn off \fBOptimize iPhone Storage\fR. With it on, many full\-resolution originals are only in iCloud and can't be copied by cable; unroll reports them as "not available on the device".
.IP \(bu 2
Connect the iPhone by cable and unlock it. The first time, tap \fBTrust This Computer\fR and enter your passcode. Keep it unlocked while a sync runs.
.SS On the Mac
.IP \(bu 2
Connect the drive you want to copy to. Any drive Finder shows works: an external SSD, a network share, or a folder on this Mac's internal disk.
.IP \(bu 2
External drives should be formatted as APFS or Mac OS Extended (Journaled).
.IP \(bu 2
Run \fBunroll\fR in a terminal. On the first launch, a wizard sets up your first sync task.
.SS Permissions
.IP \(bu 2
To add files to Apple Photos, macOS asks once whether your terminal app may control Photos. Allow it, or change it later in System Settings → Privacy & Security → Automation.
.IP \(bu 2
Notifications are shown by Script Editor on unroll's behalf; macOS may ask to allow them.
.SH SYNC TASKS
.PP
A sync task copies new photos and videos from one iPhone or iPad to one folder. You set it up once; after that, a run copies only what isn't in the folder yet.
.SS What a task remembers
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDevice\fR: recognized by its device ID, never by its name.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBDestination\fR: a folder on a drive. The drive is recognized by its volume UUID (or a network share by its address), never by its name or where it's mounted.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBFolder structure\fR: date folders inside the destination, by default \fBYYYY/YYYY\-MM\fR (for example \fB2026/2026\-06\fR). Tokens: \fBYYYY\fR, \fBMM\fR, \fBDD\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBOriginal vs. edited\fR: originals only (default), the edited version where one exists, or both plus the edit data (AAE).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBAdd to Apple Photos\fR: off by default. See \fBunroll help photos\-referenced\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBNotification when done\fR: off by default.
.SS How new items are found
.PP
An item counts as already imported when its capture time, size and file name match an entry in the destination's index. File names alone repeat on iPhones (IMG_1234), so all three must match. The capture time is the photo's clock time, so changing the Mac's time zone doesn't make old photos look new. Files with identical content are stored once.
.SS The first run
.PP
The first run of a task shows what will be copied and asks once. Later runs start right away. It asks again only if the task's device or destination changed, or if the destination's index is empty or was rebuilt. Without a terminal, such a run stops with exit code 5; pass \fB\-\-yes\fR to confirm it.
.SS Where things are stored
.IP \(bu 2
Photos and videos: the task's destination folder.
.IP \(bu 2
Import index: \fB.unroll/\fR inside the destination folder, so files and index always stay together.
.IP \(bu 2
Tasks and settings: \fB~/.config/unroll/\fR (or \fB$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/unroll\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
Logs: \fB~/Library/Logs/unroll/\fR, one file per day, kept for 90 days.
.SS Managing tasks
.PP
In the menu, \fBMore for "…"\fR holds preview, edit, delete, rebuild index and history for a task; \fBOther tasks…\fR switches to another task or creates a new one. On the command line: \fBunroll tasks list\fR, \fBunroll tasks add\fR and \fBunroll tasks remove\fR. Removing a task never touches the files or the index in its destination.
.SH APPLE PHOTOS WITH REFERENCED FILES
.PP
unroll can add new files to Apple Photos after each sync. Photos then shows them without copying them into its library: the originals stay in the destination folder, and the library stays small.
.SS Setting it up
.IP \(bu 2
In Photos, open Settings → General and turn off \fBCopy items to the Photos library\fR. unroll can't read this setting, so it reminds you of it after each import.
.IP \(bu 2
Turn on \fBAdd to Apple Photos\fR for the task: More for "…" → Edit task.
.IP \(bu 2
Files go into the library Photos has open. To use another library, hold Option while opening Photos.
.SS What happens during a run
.IP \(bu 2
After copying, unroll hands the new files to Photos in one import, so each sync is one entry under Imports. Live Photos are imported together and stay paired. Edit data (AAE) isn't imported on its own.
.IP \(bu 2
Photos comes to the front during the import and the terminal comes back afterwards. Photos doesn't report progress, so unroll shows the time spent.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBLet Photos check for duplicates\fR (on by default) skips photos already in the library, for example from an earlier import straight from the iPhone. When Photos finds some, it asks in its own dialog and waits for your answer, also in runs from Shortcuts. Turn the check off for unattended runs if your library has no older copies.
.IP \(bu 2
If Photos can't be controlled, the files are remembered and added on the next run.
.IP \(bu 2
To pause Photos for all tasks, use \fBAdding to Apple Photos: allowed / paused\fR under Help & settings → Settings. Files copied meanwhile are added once it's allowed again.
.SS Things to know about referenced files
.IP \(bu 2
Thumbnails stay visible when the drive is disconnected. Opening, editing or exporting an original needs the drive connected.
.IP \(bu 2
Don't move or rename folders in the destination after the import, or Photos loses the link.
.IP \(bu 2
Referenced files aren't part of Photos backups and don't sync with iCloud Photos. Back up the destination itself.
.IP \(bu 2
To see which photos are stored inside the library instead, create a Smart Album with the condition "Photo is not Referenced".
.SH TROUBLESHOOTING
.SS The iPhone isn't found, or is "locked or doesn't trust this Mac"
.IP \(bu 2
Use a cable that carries data, unlock the iPhone, and tap \fBTrust This Computer\fR when asked.
.IP \(bu 2
If it never asks: Settings → General → Transfer or Reset iPhone → Reset → Reset Location & Privacy, then connect again.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBunroll scan\fR lists the connected devices and their items without copying anything.
.SS Items are "not available on the device"
.PP
Their originals are only in iCloud, because \fBOptimize iPhone Storage\fR is on. Turn it off and give the iPhone time to download the originals, then run the task again. These items count as failed (exit code 1) so scripts notice.
.SS Photos arrive as JPEG instead of HEIC
.PP
Set Settings → Photos → Transfer to Mac or PC → \fBKeep Originals\fR on the iPhone.
.SS "The destination isn't connected"
.PP
The task's drive isn't mounted. unroll recognizes it by its volume UUID, so a different drive with the same name won't be used. Network shares must be mounted in Finder first.
.SS A sync was interrupted
.PP
Just run the task again. An interrupted file is never recorded as imported; the next run cleans it up and copies it again. Ctrl\-C finishes the current file before stopping.
.SS The index is lost or damaged
.PP
Use \fBRebuild index\fR (More for "…" in the menu) or \fBunroll rebuild\-index\fR. It reads every file in the destination and records it again; files are never changed. The next run asks once before copying.
.SS Files aren't added to Apple Photos
.IP \(bu 2
Check that \fBAdd to Apple Photos\fR is on for the task (Edit task), and that adding isn't paused in Settings.
.IP \(bu 2
If macOS didn't allow it: System Settings → Privacy & Security → Automation → your terminal app → Photos. The files are added on the next run.
.IP \(bu 2
If Photos copied the files into its library, "Copy items to the Photos library" was on in Photos → Settings → General.
.SS macOS asks for permission again after an update
.PP
A build from source gets a new signature after \fBbrew upgrade\fR, so macOS may ask once more to allow controlling Photos. Allow it again.
.SS Where the logs are
.PP
\fB~/Library/Logs/unroll/\fR, one file per day. Each run lists every copied or failed file.
.SH EXIT CODES
.PP
unroll ends with one of these codes, so scripts and Shortcuts can react. With \fB\-\-json\fR, the last line also contains it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB0\fR: success, including when there was nothing new to copy.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB1\fR: finished, but some items failed or weren't available on the device, or they couldn't be added to Apple Photos.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB2\fR: the destination isn't available: its drive isn't connected, it's read\-only, full, or another run is using it.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB3\fR: the iPhone isn't connected, is locked, or doesn't trust this Mac.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB4\fR: the task wasn't found, or the configuration is invalid.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB5\fR: the run needs confirmation, but there's no terminal to ask in. Run it once in a terminal, or pass \fB\-\-yes\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB130\fR: stopped with Ctrl\-C after finishing the current file.
.SH KEYBOARD SHORTCUTS
.IP \(bu 2
\fB↑ ↓\fR (or \fBk j\fR): move in a menu.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBEnter\fR: choose. In the main menu, Run is preselected, so Enter runs the task.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB← →\fR (or \fBh l\fR), \fBy\fR, \fBn\fR: answer a yes/no question.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB/\fR: filter a long list; \fBEsc\fR stops filtering.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBCtrl\-C\fR during a sync: finish the current file, then stop. Nothing incomplete is recorded.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.config/unroll/\fR
Sync tasks and settings (or \fI$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/unroll\fR).
.TP
\fI<destination>/.unroll/\fR
The import index, next to the files.
.TP
\fI~/Library/Logs/unroll/\fR
One log file per day, kept for 90 days.
